.TH FKSLACK 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkslack, fkfit \- control the buffer size of an fkstring
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkslack(fkstring *" fks ", size_t " n );
.BI "fkstring *fkfit(fkstring *" fks );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkslack ()
ensures that
.I fks
has room for at least
.I n
more bytes, so that appending or inserting up to
.I n
bytes will not reallocate.
If the buffer is smaller than
.BR fkstrlen ( fks )\~+\~ \fIn\fP\~+\~1
bytes, it grows to exactly that size; otherwise it is left alone.
.PP
.BR fkfit ()
shrinks the buffer of
.I fks
to exactly
.BR fkstrlen ( fks )\~+\~1
bytes, releasing any slack.
Use it on a string that has finished growing and will be kept for a while.
.PP
Both functions set the buffer size directly, bypassing the growth factor,
shrink threshold, and minimum size described in
.BR fkstring (3).
Neither changes the contents or length of
.IR fks .
.PP
Both do nothing to an empty
.BR fkstring ,
which never has a buffer.
To preallocate a new string, use
.BR fkalloc (3).
.SH RETURN VALUE
.IR fks ,
or NULL if
.I fks
is NULL.
.SH ERRORS
These functions do not return on allocation failure, nor when
.BR fkstrlen ( fks )\~+\~ \fIn\fP\~+\~1
overflows
.BR size_t ;
see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH NOTES
Slack is not permanent.
A later
.BR fkstrtrunc (3),
.BR fkremove (3),
or trim that leaves the buffer past the shrink threshold returns
the memory.
.PP
Either function may move the buffer, invalidating pointers into it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring *s = fkstrnew("Log:");
\&
fkslack(s, 4096);            /* one realloc() here... */
for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    fkstrcatf(s, " %d", v[i]);  /* ...and none while it fits */
fkfit(s);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkalloc (3),
.BR fkstrtrunc (3)
